    <h1 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl lg:text-[28px] font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight mt-0 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Favicon Size Guide: Every Size You Actually Need
    </h1>

    <AuthorByline publishDate="2026-08-22" updatedDate="2026-09-28" />

    <div class="text-body text-base sm:text-[17px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>"Favicon size" doesn't have one answer, because a favicon isn't one file anymore. Modern browsers, iOS home screens, Android, and Google's own search results each expect a different pixel size, and a single 16&times;16 icon that covered everything in 2005 now looks broken or blurry in most of those places. Below is every size actually in use today, followed by the format question, which is where most guides stop and most people actually go wrong.</p>

        <h2>What Is the Favicon Size? Every Size You Need</h2>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead class="bg-canvas-soft text-ink">
                    <tr>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Size</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Used for</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Typical filename</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">16&times;16</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Classic browser tab icon</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>favicon.ico</code> (bundled)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">32&times;32</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Taskbar, bookmarks, retina browser tabs</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>favicon.ico</code> (bundled) or <code>favicon-32x32.png</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">48&times;48</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Windows site shortcuts; the size Google itself displays in search results</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>favicon.ico</code> (bundled)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">180&times;180</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Apple Touch Icon, iOS/iPadOS home screen</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>apple-touch-icon.png</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">192&times;192 and 512&times;512</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Android home screen and PWA manifest icons</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>icon-192.png</code>, <code>icon-512.png</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p>A <code>favicon.ico</code> file can bundle several of those raster sizes (commonly 16, 32, and 48) into one container, and the browser picks whichever frame fits the context, which is why you only need one <code>&lt;link rel="icon"&gt;</code> tag for it despite covering three sizes. SVG favicons sidestep the whole table: an SVG has no fixed pixel size, so one file scales cleanly to any of the above, though older Safari and legacy browsers still need a raster fallback alongside it. This site's own setup is exactly that combination: a <code>favicon.svg</code> as the scalable source, plus a <code>favicon.ico</code> that, decoded, bundles a 48&times;48 and a 32&times;32 frame for browsers that don't read SVG favicons yet.</p>

        <p>One important limit on this tool specifically: PasteDownloadApp converts <strong>format</strong>. It does not resize an image's pixel dimensions. Say you need a 512&times;512 icon from the table above, but your source photo is 4000&times;3000. This tool will give you a 4000&times;3000 file in whatever format you choose, not a resized one; resize first in an image editor, then use this tool for the format and transparency step, which is what it's genuinely useful for. Here is what each use case needs, backed by a real conversion of an actual 512&times;512 app icon.</p>

        <VideoLink
